I'm running the mighty nginx web server, version 1.24.0, which is handling all the HTTP requests coming into this server. It's configured to listen on port 80, and I've also set up a reverse proxy to handle HTTPS requests on port 443, using a self-signed certificate for now.

Here's a quick rundown of the nginx configuration:
```
http {
    ...
    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name example.com;
        ...

        location / {
            index index.html;
            root /var/www/html;
        }
    }
}
```
And for HTTPS, I've got a separate configuration block:
```
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name example.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.key;
    ...
}
```
This setup allows me to serve both HTTP and HTTPS content with a single server instance.

